Today's investigation changes the design substantially. The earlier framing assumed node 0's failure was gripper occlusion that a better angle fixes. We now know most of it isn't — so the algorithm's first job is classifying the failure, because only some classes are NBV's problem.

Updated failure taxonomy (in pic)



The reconciliation that E4 got wrong: ray-cast "visible" ≠ trackable. A node can be geometrically unoccluded yet have zero mask pixels (marker color, or grazing/foreshortened cable). The predictor must
hold both signals and reason about their disagreement.

What to log from both pipelines

Per frame, on a common timeline, for every run (V4 and V5, paint-on and paint-off):

Tracker-state (what actually happened):
- per-node red-mask pixel count in a window around each projected node — the real visibility signal
- per-node node_support P1 (already published by the C++ node)
- CPD-LLE per frame: iterations-to-converge, did-not-converge flag (needs the diagnostic topic / rosout parse — the "E0" step)
- tracking-step time; downsampled point-cloud size
- per-node inter-frame displacement ‖Yₜ−Yₜ₋₁‖
- polyline: per-segment length ratio, total-length ratio
- per-node turning angle (discrete curvature) and its frame-to-frame rate
- per-node tracked−GT error (the label; sim only)

Kinematic / geometric (what a real robot knows without vision):
- camera world pose per frame (from schedule/FK); intrinsics, FOV, depth range
- r2, r3 joint states → gripper + link poses (occluder geometry)
- r2, r3 planned trajectory (future occluder geometry) — from the waypoint script
- per-node ray-cast label {VISIBLE, OCCLUDED, OUT_OF_FRAME} + occluder geom name (like
- per-node analytic self-occlusion, now and H seconds ahead (camera→node ray vs arm capsules, using the planned trajectory)
- per-node material tag: is this node on a marker segment? (blind-spot flag, from the
- static scene mesh (box, table); cable GT node positions (sim; substitute tracker estimate in deployment)

Run metadata (constant per run, for cross-run attribution):
- cable length / node count / segment length / radius
- CPD-LLE params (BETA, LAMBDA, LLE_WEIGHT, MAX_ITER, tau_vis, DLO_PIXEL_WIDTH); HSV bands
- camera schedule (poses, hold windows, rail); publish rate
- carry-motion descriptor (monotonic vs zigzag; waypoints; speeds)

Failure predictor — inputs / outputs

Inputs (per-frame feature vector + trailing-window deltas):
- convergence health: did-not-converge rate over last N frames, iterations trend towar
- min per-node P1 and min per-node mask support (value, which node, trend)
- per-node ray-cast label + occluder class (arm / box / none) + analytic self-occlusio
- the reconciliation feature per at-risk node: visible+marker → blind-spot · visible+cable+~0 px → foreshortening/grazing · occluded-by-arm → geometric · out-of-frame → framing
- shape stress: max segment stretch, total-length ratio, per-node curvature & its rate
- per-node inter-frame jump outliers; point-cloud size vs nominal

Outputs:
- per-frame risk ∈ [0,1]; which node(s); estimated frames-to-divergence
- failure class ∈ {blind-spot, geometric-occlusion, foreshortening, joint-solve-degeneracy, slip}
- recommended mitigation ∈ {none, invoke-NBV, repaint-markers/widen-HSV, enable-FK-pri driven by the class, not by risk alone

The class output is the point. "Risk high + class = blind-spot" → recommend repaint, n + class = geometric-occlusion" → call NBV.

NBV planner — inputs / outputs

Inputs:
- current (or lookahead-predicted) cable state Y (N×3 world)
- r2, r3 arm + gripper poses now and over the horizon (planned trajectory)
- static scene mesh; r1 reachable pose set (rail × IK × collision — including against moving r2/r3, per E4b)
- camera intrinsics / FOV / depth range
- per-node trackability weight: high for struggling nodes and mask-visible endpoints; zero for blind-spot-limited nodes (don't optimize a view for a node the mask can't see) unless paired with repaint
- the predictor's failure class (so it knows what it's solving)

Objective (revised for today's lessons):
- pixel-footprint visibility, not single-ray — project each node's cable cross-section (radius), count would-be-cable pixels surviving occlusion + in-frame + within depth + above DLO_PIXEL_WIDTH.
  Validate against a real render for the top candidates.
- viewing ray ⟂ local cable tangent (anti-foreshortening) — grazing angles zeroed out mask support today even when "visible"
- endpoint priority (only for mask-visible endpoints); projected node spacing ≥ thresh
- lookahead stability: integrate the score over the horizon, pick the pose that stays good longest
- move cost: transit-gap duration, joint travel, limit proximity, collision along the tch hysteresis
- reachability as a hard constraint

Outputs:
- best reachable pose (pos, quat, solved qpos); per-node predicted visibility gain vs transit cost
- an explicit "no reposition materially helps" verdict when the best candidate doesn't beat current — E4 should have returned this instead of a false-positive "99% visible"

The cross-pipeline data you actually need

You now have four conditions: {V4, V5} × {paint on, paint off}. Use them as:
- V4 paint-off = negative control: blind spot present all run, no runaway → predictor mask support alone
- V5 paint-off = positive: predictor must fire in the ~5 s runway between did-not-converge onset (t≈24–27) and unrecoverable error, and label class = joint-solve-degeneracy (blind-spot-triggered)      - paint-on vs off on each = feature attribution: how much of the risk each feature exp output flips correctly (blind-spot → resolved)
- V4 vs V5 at the equivalent carry instant = isolates the trigger features: what's in V5's t≈24–27 that isn't in V4's — curvature-rate (zigzag), sustained single viewpoint, convergence-collapse onset
Gap: today's runs are almost all blind-spot mode. You have ~0 clean geometric-occlusion failures — the case NBV actually exists for. Before the NBV objective can be validated, you need to deliberately generate a few: pick 2–3 camera poses that put an arm squarely between the camera and  segment during the carry, run them, confirm mask support drops there, and check thatNBV recovers a pose that restores it.
